    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A great analogy to illustrate this concept is the \"gobbling up of a soup\" by a hungry goblin. Imagine a goblin sitting at a table, slurping down a bowl of soup with a long, curved spoon. As the goblin moves the spoon through the soup, it creates a pressure differential, with the pressure dropping as the spoon approaches the surface of the soup and increasing as it moves away.\n\nNow, as the goblin moves the spoon downward, it's not just the spoon that's moving; the soup itself is also being deflected downward. This deflection is a result of the momentum transfer from the spoon to the soup. The spoon's downward motion is a result of the goblin's upward motion, which is a direct consequence of the goblin's momentum being transferred to the spoon.\n\nIn a similar way, when an airfoil moves through the air, it creates a pressure differential, with the pressure dropping above the airfoil and increasing below it. As the airfoil deflects downward, it's not just the airfoil that's moving; the air itself is also being deflected downward. This deflection is a result of the momentum transfer from the airfoil to the air, which is a direct consequence",
